INTERNATIONAL MONETARY FUND – NOTICE OF PRIVILEGES AND IMMUNITIES, DISPUTE RESOLUTION AND OTHER PROCEDURAL TERMS*

This notice (“Notice”) supplements the MIT license (the “License”) where the License is identified on the IMF’s landing page on GitHub or in any repository as being applicable to code material owned by the International Monetary Fund (the “IMF”) and made available under that License (the “Code”). In this Notice, “Licensee” means any person or entity that exercises any rights granted under the License with respect to the Code. The License is subject to, and shall be read together with, the terms of this Notice:

1. Privileges and Immunities. Article IX of the Articles of Agreement of the International Monetary Fund, as incorporated into the laws of the United States and the District of Columbia by the Bretton Woods Agreements Act (22 U.S. Code Section 286h), provides, among other things, that (i) the IMF, its property and its assets, wherever located and by whomsoever held, are immune from every form of judicial process; (ii) the IMF, its assets, property, income, and its authorized operations and transactions, are immune from all taxation and from all customs duties; and (iii) the IMF is immune from liability for the collection or payment of any tax or duty. No privileges or immunities of the IMF are waived under the License, and any purported waiver of any privileges or immunities, including any purported submission to the jurisdiction of any court, shall be void.

2. Dispute Resolution. All disputes in connection with the License in respect of the Code shall be settled by binding arbitration in the District of Columbia, USA under the Commercial Arbitration Rules of the American Arbitration Association (the “Rules”), except, with respect to the IMF, to the extent the Rules involve any waiver of privilege or immunity. Where neither party’s claim exceeds $75,000, then the Expedited Procedures of the Rules shall apply, and the case shall be decided by a single arbitrator. Otherwise, the case shall be decided by a panel of three arbitrators, and each party shall appoint one arbitrator. The two arbitrators so appointed shall then appoint a third arbitrator who shall act as chairperson of the panel. If a claim or dispute would have been barred by a time limitation had it been asserted in a court of the District of Columbia, then the arbitrator or panel, as applicable, shall declare the claim or dispute to be extinguished. Each party agrees to implement any requirements of the arbitrator or panel directed to it in accordance with the Rules. No award or procedural order made in the arbitration shall be published. It is understood and agreed that the submission of a claim or dispute to arbitration shall not excuse performance under the License and shall not be considered to be a waiver of the privileges or immunities of the IMF.

3. Governing Law. The License shall in all respects be interpreted under, and governed by, the laws of the District of Columbia, USA, including, without limitation, as to validity, interpretation and effect, without giving effect to District of Columbia conflicts of laws principles.

4. Precedence. In the event of any inconsistency between this Notice and the terms of the License, the terms of this Notice shall prevail.

*This Notice is purely procedural. It reserves privileges and immunities enjoyed by the International Monetary Fund as an intergovernmental organization, without adding any restrictions to the permissions granted by the License.
